## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates understanding of lock-free inter-process communication under simple multicore constraints and cache coherence protocols. It distinguishes candidates who know the mechanics of atomics from those who understand visibility and ordering guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- Lock-free ring buffers with atomic indices
- Acquire/release semantics for safe cross-core visibility
- Wrap-around via power-of-two masking
- Contention handling and backoff strategies

## Code Example
```c
#define SIZE 1024  // power of two
#define MASK (SIZE - 1)

_Atomic size_t head = 0;
_Atomic size_t tail = 0;
type_t buffer[SIZE];

void enqueue(type_t value) {
    size_t h = atomic_fetch_add(&head, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    buffer[h & MASK] = value;
    atomic_thread_fence(memory_order_release);
}

type_t dequeue(void) {
    size_t t = atomic_load(&tail, memory_order_acquire);
    type_t value = buffer[t & MASK];
    atomic_fetch_add(&tail, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
    return value;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to integrate a simple QoS mechanism with a basic I/O scheduler, showing end-to-end flow from I/O request to wakeup. Demonstrates reasoning about blocking, concurrency, and fairness under bursts.

## Key Concepts

- Token bucket I/O throttling
- Per-process QoS guarantees
- Blocking, wakeup, and race avoidance
- Simple ready-queue scheduling and aging for fairness
- Testability with burst scenarios

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal sketch: token bucket I/O
typedef struct { int tokens; int capacity; } bucket;
bucket buckets[MAX_PROCS];

int io_request(int pid, int bytes){
  if (buckets[pid].tokens >= bytes){
    buckets[pid].tokens -= bytes;
    submit_disk(pid, bytes);
    return 1;
  } else {
    enqueue_io(pid, bytes);
    block(pid);
    return 0;
  }
}

void refill_all(int per_tick){
  for (int i = 0; i < MAX_PROCS; i++){
    buckets[i].tokens = min(buckets[i].tokens + per_tick, buckets[i].capacity);
    wakeup_if_waiting(i);
  }
}
```

